WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.019
During the first season of The Mandalorian,
Star Wars fans were shown the galaxy from

00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:09.330
a whole new perspective with many of the show's
characters providing answers to some of the

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.540
franchise's oldest questions... and others
posing brand new questions for fans to ponder.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.520
In Chapter 3, Star Wars fans got a lot more
Mandalorian than they bargained for.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.680
After turning Baby Yoda over to the Client,
Mando earns a treasure trove of Beskar steel,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.240
which he brings to the mysterious Armorer
seen in the first episode.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.280
This draws the attention of the other helmeted
Mandalorians, and one of them isn't too happy

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.500
with Mando acquiring the Beskar by working
for remnants of the Empire.

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:42.920
This scene reveals a little more about the
Mandalorian culture while still raising a

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.500
few questions about their creed.

00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:49.340
During the brief melee between Mando and his
brethren, it's revealed that the Mandalorians

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.540
follow a strict warrior code.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:52.920
"This is the way."

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.800
Take Mando's refusal to take the Mudhorn as
his signet, because his victory over the creature

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.760
wasn't noble enough.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.799
Or the fact that the other Mandalorians compromise
their hideout to fight for Mando and his charge

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.240
in spite of their earlier hostility towards
him.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.600
These incidents speak volumes to the Mandalorians'
complex, unyielding sense of honor.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.560
During Chapter 5 of The Mandalorian, the show's
protagonist is forced to land on Tatooine

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.620
after his ship is damaged in battle with another
bounty hunter.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.160
The Razorcrest's landing awakens a small squad
of slapstick droids whose design you may remember

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:32.080
from The Phantom Menace, in which the same
pit droids appeared in Watto's shop.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.120
While dormant, the droids almost look like
metal turtles.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.840
But when Jar Jar Binks unintentionally brings
one to life, the young Anakin lets him know

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.000
that pressing their prominent noses puts them
back into their dormant state.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.840
More of these comical droids appear in the
podrace sequence, during which they act as

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.900
clumsy, Three-Stooges-esque mechanics for
the racers.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:57.400
Mando insists the human mechanic Peli not
allow any of her pit droids near the Razorcrest,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.580
and he's got a couple good reasons why.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.260
Obviously, he doesn't want any of them finding
Baby Yoda, and he's not exactly fond of droids

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:05.760
in general.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.560
But Mando's also probably aware of how remarkably
bad the droids tend to be at their jobs.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.620
In Episode I's pod race, the droids do far
more harm than good when their antics get

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.510
one of their colleagues sucked into the pod
engine of Ody Mandrell, scrapping the diminutive

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.100
alien's chances at a win.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:29.400
Ming-Na Wen appears in Chapter 5 as the vicious
Fennec Shand, an assassin who has been on

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.880
the run ever since the New Republic imprisoned
many of her old employers.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.100
It's not clear exactly who those employers
are, but Mando guesses they could've included

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:38.820
the Hutts.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.360
That seems likely, considering she's in hiding
on Tatooine, which has historically found

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.760
itself under Hutt control.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.319
But the very fact that Shand is on the run
suggests that the Hutts may no longer be in

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.160
charge and that's kind of surprising, even
considering the recent fall of the Empire.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.040
Sure, Jabba the Hutt died during the events
of Return of the Jedi, but he was hardly the

00:02:59.040 --> 00:02:59.760
only Hutt.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.260
You would expect another would come in to
fill the power vacuum left by the crime lord's

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:04.760
death.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:11.000
The Hutts operated on Tatooine free from interference
during the reign of the old Republic, so why

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.820
would that change with the new one?

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.500
Of course, it's entirely possible that, with
Luke Skywalker's ties to Tatooine and his

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:22.690
crucial role in defeating the Empire, the
New Republic gave the desert planet a little

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.940
more attention than other Outer Rim worlds.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.019
After Mando and Baby Yoda finally blast away
from Tatooine, the show introduces another

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.640
character or, at least, their feet.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.300
Not much is seen of the shadowy figure who
approaches Fennec Shand's body, but it's a

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.280
good guess that they're stalking Mando and
baby Yoda.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.310
And considering the prominent cape and the
Tatooine setting, it's not beyond the realm

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:50.700
of possibility that this is a cameo by someone
fans have been hoping would show up in the

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.420
series: Boba Fett.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.040
Now, Boba Fett supposedly died in Return of
the Jedi when he was knocked into the Sarlacc

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:57.540
Pit.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.900
But in some versions of the now non-canon
Legends universe, Boba Fett's armor helped

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.520
him survive and eventually escape the Sarlacc.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.360
Many fans have been hoping the legendary bounty
hunter would eventually show up in The Mandalorian,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.900
and it's possible this is who is seen examining
Shand's corpse at the end of Chapter 5.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.260
Of course, it goes without saying that this
isn't a definitive cameo by any means.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.240
After all, it isn't as if Mando and Boba Fett
are the only Star Wars characters with capes

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.800
and any number of characters could be behind
this sneaky appearance.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:35.460
The shadowy figure could easily be Giancarlo
Esposito's mysterious, caped Moff Gideon,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:40.660
for example, or someone in his employ — and
let's not forget that Darth Maul has a tendency

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.360
to show up when you least expect him.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:48.000
Really, it could be anyone, and even the show's
first season finale doesn't offer up any answers

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:48.780
to this question.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.520
But considering the secretive nature behind
this character's identity and the planet they're

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.920
first seen on, it's clear that the show's
creative team at least wants the audience

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.000
to think this could be Fett.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.780
The droid known as Zero is the Razorcrest's
pilot during Chapter 6 but there's more to

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.780
this sinister droid than meets the eye.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.360
For one, he's voiced by critically-acclaimed
director and actor Richard Ayoade, best known

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.479
for playing Moss on The IT Crowd.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.940
"I came here to to drink milk and kick ass.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.740
And I’m I’ve just finished my milk."

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.620
But there's also the type of droid Zero is.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.920
It seems likely that Zero is an RA-7 droid,
or something very much like it.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:36.700
These droids were largely used by the Empire
as spies, and in spite of some of his shortcomings,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.160
Zero does come off as slightly more cunning
than the rest of the crew.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.580
After all, he's the only one who thinks to
examine the recording of Greef Karga's message

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.920
in case there's something important about
it which, obviously, there is.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:54.700
It's also interesting that Zero's immediate
reaction to Baby Yoda is to try to kill him.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:59.080
Not to capture him and sell him to the Guild,
but simply to kill him.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.080
This could easily be down to his former Imperial
programming as a type of droid that was used

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:10.650
specifically on clandestine missions, he may
know exactly what Baby Yoda is, and what he's

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:11.280
capable of.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.040
Credited only as "New Republic Soldier," the
sole free human aboard the prison ship before

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.960
Mando and his crew arrive doesn't seem particularly
impressive at first.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:25.740
He's as green as Baby Yoda, but without the
Force skills, and seems about as capable of

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.400
pulling the trigger on Mando and his crew
as he is of pulling off that helmet.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.980
So it might surprise you to find that this
guy is actually Darth Vader.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.380
Well, kind of.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.260
Playing the New Republic Soldier is Matt Lanter,
an actor with a whole lot of voice work under

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:46.210
his belt, including voicing none other than
Anakin Skywalker on the animated series The

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:47.240
Clone Wars.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.759
He voiced the would-be Sith lord again in
Star Wars: Rebels and Star Wars: Forces of

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:57.289
Destiny, as well as in video games such as
Star Wars: Battlefront II and a number of

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:58.680
the LEGO Star Wars games.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:04.860
The sister/brother duo of Xi'an and Qin aren't
exactly the most sympathetic characters in

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:05.700
The Mandalorian.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.770
Xi'an comes off as the kind of killer who
would just laugh as she tears her victims

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:15.310
to pieces, while Qin betrays just about everyone
in Chapter 6, including the crew sent to break

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.300
him out — and even his own sister.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.420
However, it does seem like the pair have good
reasons to have turned cold and heartless.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:28.060
Xi'an and Qin are Twi'leks, a race known for
being targets for galactic slave traders.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.680
Female Twi'leks in particular are sought out
because they're considered physically attractive

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:38.229
to many different races, and you might remember
seeing female Twi'lek slaves in Jabba's Palace

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:42.000
in Return of the Jedi or in Mos Espa in The
Phantom Menace.

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:47.040
Xi'an hints that she and Mando have been intimate
in the past, and even implies she might have

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:51.740
seen him without his helmet but she doesn't
seem like a particularly reliable source,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:52.240
either.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.520
While Qin is seemingly dead by the end of
Chapter 6, Xi'an is still alive on the prison

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:01.770
ship, and it could be that more about her
and Mando's past is revealed at some point

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:02.880
later in the series.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:07.419
By the time Mando returns to the space station
after springing Qin from the New Republic

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:11.660
prison ship, he knows he's got nothing but
more betrayal waiting for him.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.830
He takes the tracker that was triggered by
the prison guard before his death and plants

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:16.860
it on Qin.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.560
Once Mando leaves the station, Ran orders
a pilot to kill him, but the waiting gunship

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:23.760
never gets a chance.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:29.400
Qin discovers the tracker and moments later,
three X-Wing fighters appear and open fire

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.580
on the hostile vessel.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:35.210
But the three pilots who attack the space
station are all used to working on the other

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:36.200
side of the camera.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:40.400
They are Rick Famuyiwa, Dave Filoni, and Deborah
Chow.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:45.920
All three have directed episodes of The Mandalorian,
with Famuyiwa directing this very episode.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:50.360
Meanwhile, both Filoni and Famuyiwa have writer
and producer credits on the series.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.380
Filoni has been with the Star Wars universe
for a while.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.220
In fact, he oversees Lucasfilm's entire animation
division.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:02.640
Before The Mandalorian, he worked as a developer
and supervising director of The Clone Wars,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.100
Star Wars Resistance, and Star Wars: Rebels.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.560
When Mando returns to the planet where he
first meets Cara Dune, the Rebel veteran is

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.400
having a ball brawling a spiky-headed opponent.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:17.620
Cara's opponent is a Dathomirian Zabrak a species whose most famous member

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.780
is Darth Maul.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.851
The resilient Sith lord killed Qui-Gon
Jinn way back in The Phantom Menace, and was

00:09:23.851 --> 00:09:28.690
seemingly cut in half by Obi-Wan Kenobi, but
miraculiously survived and showed up in both

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.280
The Clone Wars at the end of Solo: A Star
Wars Story.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.040
Because Sith lords are super tough to kill,
right Palpatine?

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:36.940
Too soon?

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.500
Feels too soon… anyway…

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:44.100
This Zabrak's name isn't revealed, but in
spite of Cara Dune getting the best of him,

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.100
he must be a resilient sort as well.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:51.260
The Sith wiped out much of their race during
the Clone Wars in retaliation for an assassination

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:52.740
attempt on Count Dooku.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.870
Whether this Zabrak or his parents were lucky
and happened to be off-world or if they managed

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.600
to escape the Sith's punishment by other means,
he's likely one of the last of his people.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:06.940
It may be a running joke that stormtroopers
aren't very good at hitting their targets,

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:11.600
but the kernels of truth that make those jokes
funny don't apply to the Death Troopers.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:16.610
In Chapter 7 of The Mandalorian, the troopers
in black armor who show up outside the Client's

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:22.160
compound are Death Troopers elite Imperial
soldiers specializing in stealth and trained

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.000
to achieve maximum body counts.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:28.950
One of their main tasks was guarding high-ranking
members of the Empire, so the fact that a

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:34.210
squad of these deadly warriors precedes Moff
Gideon's arrival on Navarro speaks to just

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.080
how influential Gideon is.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.860
If the Death Troopers look familiar, they
should.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.300
The Death Troopers kept Orson Krennic company
through most of Rogue One: A Star Wars Story.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:49.261
They can be seen in the spin-off's opening
sequence, when Krennic finds the Erso

00:10:49.261 --> 00:10:52.740
family in hiding, and they're with him on
Scarif when the heroes arrive

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.140
to get the Death Star plans.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.510
The deployment of the Death Troopers to the
battlefield is one of the moments that turns

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.500
the tide against the Rebels' surprise attack
on the facility.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:07.480
In Chapter 7, The Mandalorian's main antagonist
makes his first appearance: Moff Gideon.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:12.340
And Gideon gets some more to do in the show's
finale, including making a stunning reveal

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:15.020
that calls back to Star Wars' animated shows.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.860
Some interesting details about Gideon become
clear in Chapter 8.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:23.320
First, Moff Gideon is apparently presumed
dead by much of the galaxy.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:28.190
When Mando tells his allies who is waiting
for them outside the compound, Cara says it's

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:31.860
impossible because Moff Gideon was executed
for war crimes.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.860
Second, it seems that Gideon had a position
of power on Mandalore.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:40.740
After Gideon reveals that he knows Mando's
birth name, Mando tells his allies the only

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:45.070
record of that name was in the registers of
Mandalore, and that Gideon was an officer

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:48.600
with the Imperial Security Bureau during the
Mandalorian Purge.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.460
But the biggest reveal comes after Gideon's
TIE fighter crashes on Navarro.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:59.200
Gideon cuts himself free from the crash with
the Darksaber a black lightsaber that first

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.780
appeared in the Clone Wars animated series.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.540
The Darksaber was originally held by Tarre
Vizsla, the first Mandalorian Jedi.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:11.800
The weapon changed hands a number of times
and was last seen in the possession of Sabine

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.760
Wren and Bo-Katan in Star Wars Rebels.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:20.610
It's not yet clear how Gideon got his hands
on the Darksaber but considering the man in

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.840
question, it's fair to say he probably didn't
get it by asking politely.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:29.240
Even at the end of The Mandalorian's first
season, not much is known about Greef Karga's

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:29.740
past.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:33.260
Obviously, he's a man of high rank in the
bounty hunters' guild.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:38.080
From his dealings with Mando, it appears Greef
is a cunning, intelligent bounty hunter who

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.320
will happily stab his allies in the back if
it suits his needs.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:46.770
He does show a spark of honor in Chapter 7,
however, when he saves the Child and his protectors

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:50.700
in return for Baby Yoda saving Greef's life
earlier in the episode.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:55.260
Season One's finale does offer up one big
hint about Karga's past, though.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:59.740
When Moff Gideon and his Imperial forces have
the heroes cornered, he reveals that he knows

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.060
very specific information about them all.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:08.480
Along with what he tells us about Mando and
Cara, Gideon refers to Greef Karga as a "disgraced

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:09.020
magistrate."

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:13.980
Greef being a disgraced magistrate suggests
that unlike his allies this bounty hunter

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:15.780
once walked a much different path.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:20.700
It's not clear what exactly Greef did to earn
his dishonor, though he is seen reaching for

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.900
a bottle of liquor in his most desperate hour.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:26.680
It's entirely possible that Greef has a soft
spot for a vice or two.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:33.140
"I never once touched my per diem - I go to
craft service, get some raw veggies, bacon,

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:35.140
cup of soup, baby I got a stew going!"

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:40.060
Chapter 8 opens on the pair of scout troopers
who killed Kuiil and kidnapped the child in

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:41.060
the previous episode.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.360
Waiting outside the town in which Gideon has
surrounded Mando, the troopers wait around

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:49.390
for orders — and a comical scene unfolds
with the troopers arguing about the Child,

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.460
and trying and failing to shoot at a piece
of rubble.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:53.420
"A pet?

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:54.980
OWWW!”

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:58.760
“Serves you right."

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:02.820
Although their helmets hide their faces, the
show's credits reveal that the troopers are

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.020
played by two actors more than familiar with
a little comedy.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:11.420
One of the troopers is played by former Saturday
Night Live writer and cast member Jason Sudeikis,

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:16.380
more recently known as one of the stars of
the 2017 science fiction black comedy Colossal.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:21.130
The other scout trooper is played by Adam
Pally, who Marvel fans might know as Gary

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:26.690
the Cameraman from 2013's Iron Man 3 — the
Tony Stark superfan who helps his idol with

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:28.100
his news van's hardware.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.760
At first there didn't seem to be much to say
about the enigmatic Manadalorian Armorer who

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.720
works with Mando's Beskar in the show's early
episodes.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.180
Chapter 8, however, gives a little more insight
into the Armorer's past.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:46.720
First, it's clear that the Armorer is more
than just a woman who fashions armor and weapons

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.300
she's a person of authority within the Mandalorian
culture.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:54.120
The first hints of this appear in Chapter
3, when she stops the impending brawl between

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:55.320
Mando and his brethren.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:15:00.260
In Chapter 8, when the Armorer tells Mando
he must discover the Child's unknown species

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.260
and reunite them, her word on the matter seems
final.

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:08.320
This would suggest her standing in relation
to Mando has an almost religious weight to

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:11.320
it like a priest sending a knight on a holy
quest.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:16.080
Second, it's shown that the Armorer is as
much of a warrior as she is an armorer.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:20.980
When she rebuffs Mando's insistence that she
come with him and his allies, it seems like

00:15:20.981 --> 00:15:25.380
she's content to die fighting like so many
of her fellow Mandalorians but that isn't

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:26.060
what happens.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:31.170
With nothing more than the tools of her trade,
the Armorer takes out an entire squad of fully-armed

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:33.200
stormtroopers armed with blasters.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:37.820
Don't be surprised if this particular character
shows up again somewhere down the road.

